# Workflow

```text
Grill (when the design branches) → Issue → branch → work → PR → human review → merge commit → retrospective
```

1. Grill when a requirement, the API, the wire format, or a roadmap boundary branches. Skill: `.agents/skills/grilling/SKILL.md`. Skip it when the Issue already has acceptance text and `docs/design.ja.md` settles the question. A question that rests on how a library behaves is asked after that behavior is measured. In V1c9 the Grill took it that Kestrel reports every failure to listen as `IOException`; Kestrel does so only for an address in use, and other socket errors, such as binding an address the machine does not have, stay `SocketException`, so the owner was asked again (U1, PR #233). A question that sets a limit or a count is answered after the start and the waits are measured with a record exactly at the limit. In V1d16 the shapes the Grill chose (16-4, 16-6) raised new questions once they were measured: a start from a record at the limit took about 13 seconds, then 2 seconds, against a bound of 1 second, and the copy into a new file at the change of day took 9.6 seconds while requests waited behind its lock. The count of 1,000 lines was chosen from the measured start (PR #453). A property that a time window keeps (the reclaim window after a restart, which keeps T8's seat count) is decided after the Grill lists the ways a run ends and starts again: a stop, a kill, a reload while it runs, starts one after another, a failed start, and a long stop. In V1d25 the answer to question 25-4 counted the window from the earlier license's `lease`; the independent design review found the reload to a shorter `lease`, two restarts in a row, and a failed start that it did not cover, and a needless wait after a long stop, and the owner was asked again (25-4a, 25-4b, PR #470). A title or a term of the user documentation that a Grill decides is checked against the Japanese glossary of `.cursor/rules/user-docs.mdc` before it is recommended: V1c4f's Q4 gave the Japanese page the title 「…を有効化する」, while the glossary writes activation as アクティベーション; the translator found it, and the implementation changed it (PR #297). A question whose answer rests on a tool of this repository, such as the step runner, is asked after the tool's current rules are read: in V1c4d2 the Grill recommended page flows that met five rules of `eng/docs/Test-GuideSteps.ps1` (an `os` block takes no capture, a How-to page holds no `steps:prebuilt`, a background restarts the same program, one per page, and no shell), and the design review (R1 to R5, R7) changed them and put two of them (R2, R5) to the owner again (PR #281). A check that a Grill gives to a review is one the reviewing agent can do: an agent drives no graphical browser. In V1c4d2b the design review (R7) had the first-time reader try Chrome, Edge, and Firefox, which no agent could, and the page ended up saying it was not tried (PR #285, the owner's R1). A check that needs a person is asked of the owner, or the page says it was not tried. A check that a row makes `internal` to test is first traced, in the Grill, to the readers that feed it: a check that no real input can fail is a question of its removal, not a test. In V1d29e the check that left out a machine ID not of 16 bytes could not fail, since every reader returns 16 bytes or `null`; the owner had it removed, with the reason in design §10.3 (PR #493). An option that leaves a guard to an outside tool's default (a stop timeout, a retry count, a release age) is checked, before it is recommended, against the item of `.cursor/rules/design-docs.mdc` that says a guard resting on an outside tool's setting writes that setting. In V1d30 the answer to question 30-g kept systemd's default `TimeoutStopSec`; the independent design review (D7) found it against that item, and the owner had the unit write `TimeoutStopSec=60`, since the distribution and the administrator can change the default `DefaultTimeoutStopSec` (`docs/rows/V1d30.md`, PR #532). The Grills of the rows that run next go to the owner in one round: the facts of four to six rows are measured first, and the questions are asked once, under a heading per row. Asked row by row, each Grill cost the owner one round trip, and the agent waited through each (the owner's decision of 2026-11-13, Issue #372).
2. One Issue is one PR. Create the Issue from a template in `.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/`. Blank issues are off.
3. A change to the meaning of the design is a `design` Issue that updates `docs/design.ja.md` before any implementation Issue (`.cursor/rules/design-docs.mdc`). The detail of a row that its own Grill decided may ship in that row's PR after an independent design review; that exception is in `design-docs.mdc`.
4. Acceptance text lives on the Issue. `docs/roadmap.md` keeps ID, title, Issue, and status only. Each sentence of Done when is checked against the behavior the Grill decided before it goes on the Issue: in V1c11 Done when said that the client fetches a new nonce after a bad header, while Q1 had decided that a bad header counts as none, so within the nonce's lifetime the client keeps the earlier one; the review (A2) found it, and the owner had it read as Q1 says (PR #245). A spike's result goes into the `## Result` section of the Issue body (`gh issue edit --body-file`) with the evidence and the reproduction commands, before the PR is called ready. The PR also sets its roadmap row to `done` and points **今** at the next row before it is called ready. A script that replaces a section of an Issue body finds the heading as a whole line (`^## Result$`), not as text, and compares the list of `## ` headings before and after the edit. In W0 the second update of the Result cut the body at the words "`## Result`" inside a Grill bullet and dropped the rest of the Grill and three sections; the design review found it, and the body came back from the edit history (PR #95, R1). A PR that fixes part of a later row changes that row's records in the same PR: the **フェーズ** line and the row in `docs/roadmap.md`, the row in the tables of design §20.7 and §22 with its list of points for the Grill, and a comment on that row's Issue that says what was fixed, by which test, and what is left. That Issue stays open. In V1d2 the fix for the correctness review's C1 also fixed the `elm-server` side of V1d3 (Issue #321), and the records still gave V1d3 both sides until the owner's review asked (PR #355, R1). A PR whose Issue waits for a setting the owner makes says in its body, when it opens, that the work after the setting goes to a PR of its own. PR #502 waited for the owner's Dependabot settings with `Part of #347` and was merged while the work after them was being finished; that work moved to a new PR. The **フェーズ** line of `docs/roadmap.md` is changed by a PR that adds a row or finishes one, and before that PR is marked ready, and again after each merge of `origin/main` into it, the line is read against the rows' states on `origin/main`: a row it names as left may have been finished by a PR merged in the meantime. When two open PRs change the line, one of them owns the change and the other leaves the line as it is; the owning PR writes the line as it stands after both merge, and when the other PR merges first, the owning PR reads its line again and corrects it, or goes back to draft until it does (step 6), before it can be merged. PR #532 (V1d30) put its change of the line back and left it to PR #538, whose line named V1d21 and V1d30 as the two rows left; PR #532, which set V1d30 `done`, merged first (12:47:49 UTC on 2026-11-28), and PR #538 merged 39 seconds later with V1d30 still named. PR #541 corrected the line. Before that, the line had named V1d3b after it was `done`, and PR #538 corrected it (PR #512's retrospective).
5. Branch from `origin/main` after a fetch: `type/<issue-number>-<slug>`, for example `feat/12-token-verify`. `type` is a Conventional Commits type.
6. Open the PR in the same turn that the branch is pushed. A PR opened before its verification and measurements are done, for a check that runs only on the runners (the PostgreSQL tests), is opened as a draft (`gh pr create --draft`) and marked ready when its body is complete. In M4b1 a PR opened that way read as ready to review while its body said the measurements were still running (PR #61). Right before `gh pr ready`, read the body (`gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/pulls/<n> --jq .body`) and search it for 「下書き」, 「走っている途中」 other than the line that says CI is running, and unchecked `[ ]` items, and check that the record of each review is in it. PRs #400 and #417 were marked ready while their bodies still said they were drafts waiting for CI and the independent review, with no table of the review's findings, and the owner's reviews asked for both. The check of the review's record holds for a PR of documents alone too: PR #512 (V1d28) was marked ready at 11:02 UTC on 2026-11-28 with no record of its independent review in the body, went back to draft 33 seconds later, and was marked ready again once the table of R1 to R6 was in it. A run that a draft PR's body names (a workflow run, a Dependabot run) is read with the checks of the open PRs (below), and its result goes into the body when it ends. PR #512's body named Dependabot's security update run 46025268257 as started; the run had ended with `failure` at 16:09:34 UTC on 2026-11-27 (`security_update_not_possible`), the PR stayed a draft until the next day, and the result reached the records only when the independent review (R1) found it. Use `.github/pull_request_template.md` (`.cursor/rules/pull-requests.mdc`). `Closes #N` stands alone on a line under `## Related` (`.cursor/rules/github-closing.mdc`). A PR that is not a draft is one the owner may merge: ready says that nothing more will be pushed. The owner's correction of 2026-11-28: 「普通にDraftでないPRはマージ対象になりますよ？マージしてほしくないなら、ドラフトにするか本文にきちんと書いておきなさい」. A PR with work still owed stays a draft. When more work is asked of a ready PR (a review's fix, a lesson to add, a failed check), it goes back to draft first (`gh pr ready <n> --undo`), and it is marked ready again only when that work is pushed. A PR that cannot be a draft says plainly at the top of its body not to merge it yet, and why. PRs #502 and #508 were merged while the agent was still finishing them, and PR #510 had merged by the time a lesson was to be added to it, which then went to Issue #511: all three were ready. A PR is marked ready once the local gate (Gates, below) and its reviews are done and nothing more is to be pushed, also while CI still runs; its body then says that CI is running. A check that fails is fixed when it is seen, with the PR back in draft until the fix is pushed (the owner's decisions of 2026-11-27 and 2026-11-28, Issues #487 and #511). When such a PR merges before its checks end, their result is written after the merge in a comment on the PR, not in its body (`.cursor/rules/git.mdc`): the owner read PR #489 before CI had ended and asked for the result, which went in a comment after the merge. Before answering a review, and before a merge, read the checks of the pushed head (`gh pr checks <n>`): a local `./build.ps1` does not run on the runners. A PR that shows no checks is first checked for a conflict with `main` (`gh pr view <n> --json mergeable`): with a conflict GitHub makes no merge commit and starts no `pull_request` run. In PR #191 two pushes got no CI run, and the checks were run by `workflow_dispatch` before the conflict with the merged PR #189 was found. Do not wait for CI or `Measure` before the next piece of work: after a push, start the next row, review, or fix at once, and read the checks of every open PR together later. Fixing a failed check takes less time than waiting for every check; on 2026-11-13 the session waited on the checks of five PRs while no row moved, and the owner ordered it to go on without waiting. The owner said it again on 2026-11-27 (PR #488): "Do not wait for CI. After the push, go on with the next work; when a run fails, fix it then." No watch of the checks runs in the foreground or in the background before the next work, and a report is sent right after the push, with the checks named as running. A run that fails is fixed when its failure is seen; a PR is merged only after its checks pass. A report that names the state of the checks reads them first (`gh pr checks <n>`): in PR #191 the owner pointed out that CI had finished while the report still said it was running. `gh pr checks <n> --watch` started right after a push ends with exit code 1 when no check is registered yet, so a command that chains the gate, the push, and the watch reports a failure although the gate and the push passed: read the checks once before watching. It happened on the third push of PR #334. A CI log read with `gh run view --log-failed` holds its colors as text (`^[[31mfailed^[[m`), so a search for `failed <test name>` finds nothing: strip them first (`sed 's/\^\[\[[0-9;]*m//g'`). In PR #159 a count of past failures came out empty twice until they were stripped. `gh run view --job <id> --log` may name every step `UNKNOWN STEP`, so lines are picked by their content, not by the step's name: in PR #297 the log of PR #293's run, filtered by the step name, gave no line. A job's log can be read only after the whole run has ended (`gh run view --job <id> --log` says the run is still in progress), and a push to the same branch cancels the run that is in progress. When a value must come from a run's log, the next push to that branch waits for that run to end, while other work goes on: in PR #237 the time of Tutorial 4 came from the log of run 42362464212, and the fixes waited for it; the runs of 966a6bd and 9d8ee73 were cancelled by the next push. A run of the repository's default CodeQL setup cannot be re-run through the API: `gh run rerun` answers "This workflow run cannot be retried". A new run comes with the next push (the re-run button on the run's GitHub page was not tried); until then, the PR body says which check failed and why. In PR #253 `Analyze (javascript-typescript)` failed on a 504 while it downloaded the CodeQL bundle. `gh run rerun <id> --failed` runs again only the jobs that failed. When the `Report` job of `Measure` fails on a target, the measuring jobs passed, so it runs `Report` alone over the same result files and fails the same way: measure again with `gh run rerun <id>`. In PR #355 run 43150974969 was rerun once with `--failed` for nothing. When the same job fails on every PR, compare the runner image (`Runner Image`, `Version:`) and the versions of the tools the job uses with a run that passed, before reading the PR's change. From 2026-11-20 the container jobs of `release.yml` failed on every PR: `ubuntu-latest` had moved from ubuntu-24.04 with Docker 28.0.4 to ubuntu-26.04 with Docker 29.4.2, whose image store loads an image another way (`.cursor/rules/testing.mdc`, PR #463). When the `Report` fails with "no run had a normal disk" (every run's disk flush p99 above 20 ms, design §4.2), measure again once; if it fails the same way again, compare the runs' disk flush p99 with the latest `Measure` run on `main`. The flush check writes to the same disk as the product, so the cause can be the runners' disks or a change that makes the disk slow on every run (V1d31, PR #428). When the AlmaLinux builds of `release.yml` (`Build linux-x64`, `Build linux-arm64`) stop with "The .NET SDK is X, not DOTNET_SDK_VERSION Y", the runner image has brought a newer SDK patch: the container uses the runner's SDK folder, and `global.json` rolls forward to the newest patch there. Compare the image version (`Runner Image`, `Version:`) and the `setup-dotnet` line "already installed" of a job that passed and one that failed. The fix raises `DOTNET_SDK_VERSION` in `release.yml` and `measure.yml`, the tag and digest of `ALPINE_SDK_IMAGE` (the registry's `Docker-Content-Digest` of the tag), and the image's row in design §21. Images roll out over days, so on the same day a run on the old image still passes. In PR #362 two Linux builds failed this way on image 20261111.370.1 while PR #360 had passed on 20261101.360.1 (PR #365).
7. A human reviews and merges with a merge commit, not a squash merge or a rebase. Every commit of the branch then lands on `main` with its own message, so each one follows `.cursor/rules/conventional-commits.mdc` and `.cursor/rules/github-closing.mdc` from the first one, and a commit that broke them stays in the history. The owner decided it on 2026-11-28 ("merge commitでいいと思います"); PRs #536 to #541 were merged that way, each merge commit with two parents, while this rule still said to squash; `main` already held merge commits beside squash merges from PR #2 on (`git log --first-parent --merges`). An agent merges only when the owner names the PR and orders it; it merges after the checks pass, with the command and the message of `.cursor/rules/git.mdc`. Turning on a PR's auto-merge (`gh pr merge <n> --auto`) is a merge: an agent turns it on only when the owner names the PR and orders it (the owner's answer to D2 of PR #539, design §20.5).
8. After the merge, delete the remote branch when GitHub has not, and the local branch with `git branch -d` once its head is in `main`, as `.cursor/rules/git.mdc` says. When `-d` still refuses, keep the local branch and ask the owner.
9. Retrospective, below, before the next feature branch.

## Rows in parallel

Rows that touch different projects run at the same time, at most four, each with its own Issue, worktree, branch, and PR. Two rows of one group do not run together. The groups of the V1d rows: the on-premises server (`ElasticLM.Server`), the client and `elm` (`ElasticLM.Client`, `ElasticLM.Cli`), the service (`ElasticLM.Service`), verification and issuing (`ElasticLM.Licensing`, `ElasticLM.Issuing`, `ElasticLM.Leasing`), the workflows (`.github/`, `eng/`), tests and documents only, and the owner's repository settings. A row that turns out to touch another group's project waits for that group's open row. A row whose PR is ready and waits only for its merge no longer holds its group: the next row of the group starts in the turn that the PR is made ready, and a conflict between the two is solved at the merge (`.cursor/rules/git.mdc`). Such a row holds back no row of another group either. On 2026-11-27 the owner found one agent at work while rows of other groups could start. The limit of four counts rows still being worked on. When two rows that run together need the same part, it is decided before they start which row adds it and merges first, and the other row uses it after that merge. PRs #474 and #475 each wrote the same cleanup of leftover temporary files, and `UnixLeftover` became one through `InternalsVisibleTo` only after both had it. The owner decided it on 2026-11-14 (Issue #433), after V1d4b had run beside V1d17 in the service group as a one-time exception and V1d3b waited for V1d15's PR #429.

An agent per row may write the code and the tests in the row's worktree, and may draft the row's design text and its user documentation (the owner's answer to question P-1, 2026-11-13). An agent other than the one that wrote a draft reviews it independently. The session that holds the rows checks each draft against the owner's decisions, runs the reviews, opens the PRs, and keeps the roadmap. It puts every design choice that a review surfaces to the owner: question 5-6 of PR #382, questions 8-5 and 8-6 of PR #391, and question 7-6 of PR #388 came from such reviews. A brief that the session gives a row's agent says to open the PR as a draft, and to report right after the push without waiting for the checks; the PR is marked ready once the local gate and the reviews are done and nothing more is to be pushed, with its body saying that CI is running, as the brief's last step. A brief that asks more work of a ready PR has the agent turn it back into a draft first (`gh pr ready <n> --undo`) and mark it ready again when the work is pushed, and reads `gh pr view <n> --json state` before each push: a ready PR may be merged at any time (step 6, the owner's decisions of 2026-11-27 and 2026-11-28, PR #488 and Issues #487 and #511). When the draft opens right after the design commit, the brief also has the independent design review (`.cursor/rules/design-docs.mdc`, Review before the PR) run after that commit. The brief for PRs #449 and #450 said to open them ready once the body was complete; both were opened ready before CI and turned back into drafts. The brief for PR #455 said to open the draft at once, and the design review ran only while it was a draft. Every PR still changes `docs/roadmap.md`, the design tables, and the end of `docs/design-decisions.md`; the conflict that the second merge brings is solved as `.cursor/rules/git.mdc` says. Until 2026-11-13 the roadmap allowed one row at a time, and thirty rows without a dependency on each other stood in one line (Issue #372).

The facts a Grill measured go to the row's agent with the way they were measured (which runs, which period, how they were counted), and the row's record in `docs/rows/` writes both. In V1d3b the Grill's count over 600 CI runs and its 40 failures in 3,000 tries reached the implementer as figures alone, and the record could not say where they came from until the independent review (T6) asked (PR #439).

A row that adds a schema migration (`src/ElasticLM.Service/Migrations/`) takes the number after the highest one on `origin/main` and in every open PR, read when the PR is opened. When two open PRs add one, the later PR's body names the order of their merges. Two migration files with one number and different names do not conflict in git. PR #431 and PR #440 both added `0009`; the independent review of PR #440 (K9) found it, and PR #440 was renumbered `0010` after PR #431 merged.

The owner's decision to add a row goes to a small PR of its own as soon as it is made, and that PR goes to the owner for merging. PR #396 stayed open while later answers (R-6, R-7, Issues #399 and #406) were added to it, and its body and tables were written again each time. The new row's ID is chosen after reading the roadmap on `origin/main`: for Issue #444 the session thought of V1d24c, which Issue #386 already had, and the agent found it and named the row V1d24d (PR #450). Such a PR also updates the count of row IDs in design §20.7 (`.cursor/rules/design-docs.mdc`). When two PRs that add rows are open at once, each body names the other and the count it sets, so the conflict at the second merge is solved quickly: PRs #489 and #491 met at the tables and the count (127), and so did PRs #499 and #504 (130). The design's sentence takes one clause per PR ("Issue #N の PR で <ID> を足して <count> 個（… と 8 個）になり、これもすべて一致する。"), and the PR adds only its own clause, counted from `origin/main` with its own row. What it assumes of another open PR stays in the PR body and never enters the sentence, so the merge that brings the other PR in only keeps both clauses, `main`'s first, and counts the branch's clause again. PR #530 wrote its count of 134 in the sentence with the parenthesis "（Issue #521 の PR が V1d3f を足して 133 個にしたあと）", and PR #534 wrote "（同じく V1d3f のあと）"; PR #523, which added V1d3f, conflicted at three merges of `origin/main` (7c8c7e9, 1d24ee6, d341164): the first counted its clause again, from 132 to 133, after V1d3e's clause. In the other two, `main`'s clauses had been counted on the assumption that V1d3f came first, so the branch's clause had to go before them, against the order of `.cursor/rules/git.mdc`, and each parenthesis was removed by hand. Counted from `origin/main` alone, the clause of the PR that merges later goes after the other and only its count changes. A clause joined to the sentence takes no space after the `。` before it: the first merge left " Issue #521 の PR で" with a space, and the second removed it.

## Issue templates and labels

Labels have the form `<axis>:<value>`. The only axis today is `type`.

| Template   | Label           | Commit / PR type           |
| ---------- | --------------- | -------------------------- |
| `feat`     | `type:feat`     | `feat`                     |
| `spike`    | `type:spike`    | `chore` (code is scratch)  |
| `bug`      | `type:bug`      | `fix`                      |
| `refactor` | `type:refactor` | `refactor`                 |
| `test`     | `type:test`     | `test`                     |
| `design`   | `type:docs`     | `docs`                     |
| `task`     | `type:chore`    | `chore` / `build` / `ci`   |

The template is chosen by what the fix will change, read before the Issue is created: `test` is for tests alone ("without changing product code"), and a fix of product code that differs from the design is a `bug`. Issue #533 (V1d3h) was created from `test` with `type:test`, while the decision 3g-2 it came from already had the fix change `ElmServer.RemoveLeftSocketAsync` in `src/ElasticLM.Server/ElmServer.cs` (the row that PR #534 added). When the cause is found only by the work, as for a test that fails now and then, the PR takes the type of what it changes: Issue #529 was created from `test` before the cause was known, and PR #531, which changed `TlsChainSelfCheck.RunAsync`, is `fix(issuing)`.

## Gates

- `./build.ps1`: tool restore, CSharpier check, restore, then Release and Debug builds with warnings as errors, each followed by every `tests/**/*.Tests.csproj`. CI runs it on Windows, Linux, and macOS for every push to a PR.
- Before a push, the agent runs the part of it that the change can break: `dotnet csharpier check .`, the build of the solution with warnings as errors, and the tests of each project the change touches, with the tests that use it (`dotnet test --project <path>`). A change to documents or rules alone runs the format check and the build. The whole gate is CI's: a PR is merged only after its checks pass, and a failure there is fixed before anything else on that branch, when it is seen. A PR is marked ready once the local gate and its reviews are done and nothing more is to be pushed, also while CI runs; a fix it needs later goes in with the PR back in draft. Nobody waits for the checks before the next work (step 6, the owner's decisions of 2026-11-27 and 2026-11-28, PR #488 and Issues #487 and #511). Run locally before every push, the gate took about twelve minutes, and one machine runs one at a time, so rows in parallel would wait in line for it (the owner's decision of 2026-11-13, Issue #372).
- The NativeAOT publish check runs where the Issue names it (`.cursor/rules/aot.mdc`).
- After each merge, read `main`'s CI. A red `main` gets its own Issue and PR before the next merge.
- A `main` run cancelled before any job started is not a failure. It was waiting in its concurrency group, and the next merge's run took its place: "By default, any existing `pending` job or workflow in the same concurrency group will be canceled" (GitHub Docs, Workflow syntax, `concurrency`). The newer run holds the cancelled commit, so read that run instead. After PR #164, the run of PR #167's merge was cancelled this way by the run of PR #169.
- A job that no runner took ("The job was not acquired by Runner") is not a failure either: run it again. A macOS job of `main`'s CI at db7a7bb ended this way.
- A test that the PR did not change and that fails once on CI gets an Issue, after a search for one that already names it. The failed job is then run again, and the PR body says so. In PR #390 `ServerAnswerTests.ServerLicenseRequest_ProblemAnswer_GivesTheListedCodeAndNeverTheDetail` failed once on macOS (Debug) and became Issue #392. In PR #391 `Measure` run 43372657476 failed with "The response ended prematurely" and became Issue #393; a rerun of the whole `Measure` passed.

## Forbidden

- Starting implementation without an Issue number and a branch.
- Treating a roadmap title, or a file under `.dev/`, as permission to code.
- Copying acceptance text onto the roadmap.
- Adding or removing roadmap rows, or touching **Intentionally out of scope**, without a Grill. A row that the work shows to be needed is put to the owner as a question with a recommendation before the PR.
- Merging. Closing an Issue or PR without an order that names it.
- Ending a turn on a branch that has no Issue and no PR.

## Numbers

Create an Issue before its number is written anywhere: a branch name, a commit, a PR body, an ADR. Issue and PR numbers share one sequence; the next one is not predictable.

```text
# BAD — write `Closes #8` into the body, then create the Issue, which gets #9
# GOOD — create the Issue, read its number from the output, then write the body
```

Creating the Issue and using its number are separate commands. A chain such as `gh issue create ... && git switch -c docs/3-slug` writes a number before the output that gives it has been read. In D0 the agent chained the two and named the branch `docs/3-...` before reading the URL; the number happened to match (PR #4's retrospective).

## Stacked PRs

A second Issue that builds on an open PR gets its own branch and PR based on the first, from its creation: `gh pr create --base <first PR's branch>`. PR #430 was opened against `main` on top of PR #428's branch, so its diff held PR #428's commits too, until the independent review (M8) had the base changed. A stacked PR is merged only after its base has merged. GitHub can make the two PRs a stack (the event `added_to_stack` on both; PRs #505 and #526 got it at 05:21 UTC on 2026-11-28). In a stack the upper PR's base cannot be changed by hand (`gh pr edit --base` refused it for PR #526 as "part of a stack", by PR #505's retrospective comment), an agent merges the lower PR only through the asynchronous merge API (`.cursor/rules/git.mdc`), and GitHub itself changes the upper PR's base to `main` when the lower PR merges: PR #526 got `automatic_base_change_succeeded` one second after PR #505 merged. Otherwise, when the base merges, retarget the next PR to `main` first, then delete the merged branch. Either way, the merge of `origin/main` into the upper PR that follows is solved as `.cursor/rules/git.mdc` says for a stack.

## Retrospective

After a merge, in the turn that learns of it, write three points as a comment on the merged PR. Write "none" when a point is empty.

1. A lesson for `.cursor/rules/`, a skill, `docs/design.ja.md`, or one ADR under `docs/adr/`. Tacit knowledge the work relied on is a lesson: a way of measuring, a way a tool fails on this setup, a mistake the review or the owner had to catch.
2. The Issue that will carry it, if any.
3. Whether to refactor. Yes opens a `refactor` Issue that comes next. Postponing it needs a roadmap row (`.cursor/rules/defer.mdc`).

The comment is written at every merge. The lessons go into the files they name in one `chore` Issue and PR for every five merges, or at the end of the day's work, whichever comes first, without waiting for an ask. A lesson that the next row would break is written at once, in a PR of its own. On 2026-11-12 five row PRs brought four lesson PRs, each with its gate, its CI, and its merge (the owner's decision of 2026-11-13, Issue #372).

The facts of a retrospective, and of the lesson PR that carries it, are taken from the merged PR's diff, its body, and its record in `docs/rows/`, not from memory of the work. Each fact a lesson PR writes is checked there again, and where the comment is wrong the rule writes what the PR shows. The independent review of PR #527 found four facts of the comments wrong: the merge 2e41a42 of PR #520 had put the branch's row before `main`'s, not after (G1); the §5.4 table conflicted once in that merge, not twice (G2); PR #522's test calls `ClearAllPools()` itself rather than closing a pool of its own (T1); and PR #524's lesson had lost its condition on the request's version (C1). Before a lesson PR writes a fact about a library or a system, and again right before it is marked ready, it reads the Issues of the open rows whose Grill or work reads that fact again, and it writes from the record whose fact is settled. PR #536 put into `csharp.mdc`, from V1d3g's record, that a cancelled `Socket.ConnectAsync` closes the socket; the Grill of Issue #533 (V1d3h), posted 48 seconds before PR #536 merged, had read the source again and found that it does not, and PR #537 corrected the sentence (decision 3h-4; `.cursor/rules/design-docs.mdc` has the case).

When a lesson PR merges while another PR is open, check the open PR against the new rule before calling it ready. In M2 the rule for testing readers of untrusted input (PR #10) merged after PR #12 opened, and the review, not the agent, found the readers PR #12 had added without those tests.

```text
# BAD — the PR merged; the next row starts; what the row taught stays in the conversation
# GOOD — the PR merged; three points on it; the lessons join the next chore PR; then the next row
```
